INT. SUDS-N-GO LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

Humming dryers. Buzzing tube light. Rain on glass.

ROSALIND, 40s, folds towels hard. Snap. Snap. JUDE, 12, sits on a washer, sneakers swinging, doing homework nobody asked about. NELL, 70s, in a quilted coat, watches both of them from a plastic chair.

Rosalind snaps a sheet.

JUDE
You folded that one already.

ROSALIND
No.

NELL
She did, dear. Twice.

ROSALIND
I'm not talking to you, Mom.

Nell nods, patient, as if this were weather. A dryer buzzes. Nobody moves.

JUDE
Mr. Pruitt said I could stay with Grandma if --

ROSALIND
Don't.

JUDE
I'm just saying.

NELL
He could. It's a big house.

Rosalind drops a towel. Picks it up. Folds it a third time.

ROSALIND
Where's the other sock?

JUDE
Dad took it.

Rosalind's hands stop. A long beat. The tube light flickers.

NELL
(to Jude)
Honey, get me a cup of cocoa from the machine.

JUDE
It doesn't take dollars.

NELL
Then go find quarters.

Jude slides off the washer, slow, watching. He leaves. Rosalind keeps folding, fast now, jaw tight.

NELL (CONT'D)
You can stop the towels.

ROSALIND
I can't.

Nell stands, crosses to the table, lays her hand flat over Rosalind's. The towel goes still between them. Rosalind's shoulders shake once, silently.

JUDE (O.S.)
(far off, rattling a machine)
Got one! Wait, it's a button.

Rosalind laughs once, wet, and wipes her face on the towel.
